  "live_records": {
    "q-354475": {
      "dom_id": "step-q354475",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}=(5x+10)+{2}^{3}(5x+10)^{2}+{3}^{3}(5x+10)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(5x+10)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}(5x+10)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{3}(5x+10)}{{n}^{3}}| \\\\  & =|5x+10|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{3} \\\\  & =|5x+10|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5|x+2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+2|<\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{5}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
