    "q-351686": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351686",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}=(3x-6)+\\frac{(3x-6)^{2}}{(2!)^{2}}+\\frac{(3x-6)^{3}}{(3!)^{2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(3x-6)^{n+1}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}}{\\frac{(3x-6)^{n}}{(n!)^{2}}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(3x-6)^{n+1}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n!)^{2}}{(3x-6)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|3x-6|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =|3x-6|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
