    "q-351678": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351678",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}=(3x-3)+{2}^{4}(3x-3)^{2}+{3}^{4}(3x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{4}(3x-3)^{n+1}}{{n}^{4}(3x-3)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{4}(3x-3)}{{n}^{4}}| \\\\  & =|3x-3|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{4} \\\\  & =|3x-3|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x-1|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-1|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
