    "q-351685": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351685",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}=(x+1)+\\frac{{2}^{2}(x+1)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{3}^{2}(x+1)^{3}}{3!}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x+1|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n+1}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =|x+1|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
